            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The 6" chimney that co-vents the boiler and hot water tank to the outside (this will be eliminated when a high efficiency heating &amp; hot water systems are installed)

** The 6" combustion air supply pipe that provides combustion air for the boiler &amp; hot water tank (this will be eliminated when a high efficiency heating &amp; hot water systems are installed)

** Outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with upgraded weatherstripping and caulking around the perimeter of the interior door trim - or replace them)

** The electrical outlets &amp; switches in exterior walls of the house (foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates)

** The existing windows have leakage both in the opening mechanisms and around the perimeter frames (can seal with upgraded weatherstripping and caulking around the perimeter of the interior door trim - or replace them)

** If you were to consider adding more insulation to the exterior walls of your house, the contractor will install a new &amp; fully sealed air barrier wrap around the house - that helps reduce air leakage through the walls &amp; the windows would be better sealed from the exterior.



Savings:

If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 311 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. Most newer houses in Inuvik are being constructed with a minimum R35 exterior walls so (as an FYI) I modeled the energy savings if your house was to have R35 (ie: another R15 = 3" of extruded polystyrene foam board) on the exterior walls. 



Savings: By increasing your exterior wall insulation up to a minimum R35, you could potentially save $1,316 in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is strongly recommended and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval recommendation. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/energy-efficient-products/



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x6 framing with R20 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



Savings: By adding 3 (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

All the windows in this house are double pane windows - some older wood ones on the main floor &amp; some newer ones on the 2nd floor. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows with ENERGY STAR® certified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 763 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 20.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:

A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND



A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:

? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)

? Intertek Canada

? Keystone Certifications

? Labtest Certification (LC)

? QAI Laboratories

? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)



Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.



Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:

? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;

? Date of installation;

? Window make and model numbers;

? NRCan Reference Number;

? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;

? Number of windows and location of each installation;

? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);

? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and

? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.



Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <doors>

The exterior doors of your house are in reasonable shape, but in need of a weather-stripping upgrade. In case you consider replacing them, I recommend you select new ENERGY STAR® certified doors.



Rebates: By replacing the four doors in your house with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 36 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.0 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre gas-fired hot water tank. You may want to consider replacing your hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 128 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.6 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a 78.2% efficient gas-fired boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing gas-fired boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE and dedicated outside combustion air pipe for your wood stove.



Savings:

By replacing your current gas-fired boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing gas-fired boiler and installing a dedicated outside combustion air pipe for your current wood stove, you could potentially save $ 2,403 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 61.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Many homeowners now choose to install a 95% combination boiler that will also heat their hot water year round (saving on needing to install a separate appliance to heat the hot water). A 95% AFUE combination boiler qualifies for a higher $1,250 in rebates from EEIP.



While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $250 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



Note: You can still get Canada Greener Homes Grant if you were to install 90% AFUE ENERGY STAR certified natural gas-fired boiler but you will not be eligible for GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation. First though - you will need to check with your Inuvik electric utility to see if they have capacity for you to connect a Solar PV system to their grid.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front south facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,600 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,186. This system may qualify for up to $ 20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program &amp; up to $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 



To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount is based on the lower of:

1. 50% of the eligible cost of the system, up to a maximum of $20,000.

2. A calculation of $3.50 per watt that the system can produce.



Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program



NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.















</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights  Non-hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulb and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2 x clothes dryers), indicated a pressure drop of 2.9 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current gas-fired boiler and hot water tank are located on the main floor of your home and can be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - A central Heat Recivery Ventilator (HRV)

        - there are 2 kitchens &amp; one of the stoves has a range hood fan above that recirculates within the house

        - the bathroom fans are connected through the HRV

        - 2 x clothes dryers that vent to the outside



Your HRV is older and may soon be in need of an upgrade. If you choose to upgrade the HRV, please choose an Energy Star &amp; HVI certified model that has a minimum 75% efficiency.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs  Inuvik



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Nat gas - $37.21 per GJ

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered

